          "type": "content",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Shilajit <brown>Overdose</brown>",
            "subtitle": "",
            "description": "<p>While it's hard to overdose on Shilajit, it's still possible to overdo it. There are no records of people being hospitalized for taking too much, but studies in the former USSR showed no side effects even with doses up to 2500 mg a day.</p><p>Still, we don’t recommend going that high. If you take too much, your body will likely flush it out through urine, and it could make your stools turn dark.</p><p>However, taking Shilajit every day without breaks for a long time could lead to a mineral overload, causing symptoms like migraines or fatigue. You can avoid this by cycling your intake, meaning you take it for a while, then take a break.</p><p>There are also a few things to watch out for: Shilajit can sometimes cause digestive issues, allergic reactions, or interact with medications, especially those affecting blood sugar or the liver. Always consult your doctor if you’re unsure!</p>",
